// permissions test root — `cpc test` discovers the `#[test]` fns in the modules
// this imports, and the imports are the point of the file.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS, and it is the same reason `vendor/http` has one. Without it,
// `cpc test` falls back to the package's source entry, `permissions.cplus` —
// which deliberately does NOT import the backend. The two halves meet at stable
// symbols (`cplus_permissions_state_v1` and its sibling) rather than through an
// import, because the backend must import `permissions` for `State` and a cycle
// back is rejected outright (E0404). So nothing in the package's own import
// graph reaches the backend, and a test driver built from that graph would link
// against symbols nothing defined.
//
// A CONSUMER NEVER HITS THAT: a package's archive is built from a synthesized
// entry importing every module in `src/`, so the backend is in the archive an
// application links whether or not anything imported it. The test driver is the
// one build that uses the real entry.
//
// The tests themselves stay next to what they test: the portable half's in
// `permissions.cplus`, the Apple half's in `permissions_backend.cplus`.

import "./permissions" as permissions;
import "./permissions_backend" as backend;
